      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Monday, March 31, 2003 The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:16 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KIRBY: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: In this case the Paiute propose and are seeking a categorical rule that makes Indian tribes, their casinos, their commercial businesses, and their personal property immune from search warrants and subpoenas issued in connection with the investigation of crime and the prosecution of crime."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Counsel, did the tribe offer to accept as consent by the employees their employment forms?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "For instance, in a Public Law 280 State, such as California, if there is a crime committed off the reservation, frankly as well as on the reservation, the Federal Government does not have jurisdiction over that crime."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If the States do not have the ability to issue a search warrant under those circumstances and there is evidence of crime or proceeds of crime on the reservation, then that property or evidence remains immune from law enforcement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would first like to correct one misunderstanding that the Court may have been left with as a result of Mr. Kirby's comments. There is no Federal regulation or other Federal requirement that would have prevented the county or the State from sharing with the tribe the information, the welfare applications, and so on. Federal --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Reid Peyton Chambers",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are three principles of Federal law that bar this search warrant. immunity. The second is the longstanding principle that States may not infringe the right of tribes to govern themselves or internal affairs on their reservation. And the third is that there's no act of Congress that authorizes this search warrant, though two statutes, Public Law 280 and the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act, do provide a framework for some assumption of State jurisdiction over tribes and Indians on reservation -- the -- reservations."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, what does the -- does the corporation own anything?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Reid Peyton Chambers",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress has never done that for a tribe, and it's very important to note that while sovereign immunity in the tribal context is a common law doctrine developed by this Court, it has been adopted by Congress."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under law 280."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0002-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Public Law 280 confers criminal jurisdiction on California on offenses by or against Indians. That's the language of Public Law 280. It does not apply to tribes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0002-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the officers went with deadbolt cutters into the tribe's building, cut the locks, and seized the records."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Four States",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "for example, from the amicus brief filed by four States on behalf of the tribe in this case, four States that have two-thirds of the Indian reservation trust lands in the country in their borders and the majority of Indians who live on reservations within their borders."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kirby",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And under the Sixth Amendment, that person has the right to have the State issue compulsory process to obtain that exculpatory evidence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "was a question raised with regard to that, I believe by Justice Scalia, pointing out that the tribe's treasury is not at issue here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2002-02-281",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "542e0a5386dc5cdc5bd44853ad4587ac11e662578bcf4b0700cccb897694c80f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-542e0a5386dc-0003-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
